            <div type="translation" resp="#martaf" xml:lang="en">
                <p>Tomb of Boniface. The endurance of many afflictions brings the hope of salvation. May it be granted to all of us who desire eternal life to practice the patience through which many also attain heavenly life. Therefore I too, a sinner in all things, desired to flee soon from my stay in this life, and having asked for this, I obtained it after the passage of this life to righteousness. Therefore, may rest be granted to me from all this senseless life, together with my fellow servant in Christ and my most beloved mother.</p>
            </div>
            <div type="commentary" resp="#martaf">
                <p>The epitaph opens with the formula <foreign xml:lang="grc">μνημεῖον</foreign> followed by the genitive of the deceased's name (l. 1) and unfolds in a complex series of poetic images and gnomic sentences. In ll. 2-5 it is stated that endurance brings salvation, and that therefore those who aspire to eternal life must practice it. In ll. 6-9, the deceased defines himself, speaking in the first person (for which, see Agnello 1953, 79), as a sinner (l. 6 <foreign xml:lang="grc">ὁ πάμπαν ἁμαρτωλός</foreign>): this rarely occurs in Christian epitaphs, but other examples can be found (ICUR IV 10535, VI 16073, cf. Dresken-Weiland 2006, 308). Boniface desired to flee from this life and obtained what he desired through the intervention of justice (ll. 8-9 <foreign xml:lang="grc">ἐπὶ τὴν δικαιωσύνην μεταβολήν</foreign>): the concept of <foreign xml:lang="la">iustitia</foreign> is rare in Christian epitaphs. In ll. 9-11 there is a final prayer for the granting of rest at the end of a senseless life (ll. 9-10 <foreign xml:lang="grc">τοῦ ἀνεσθήτου βίου</foreign>: the adjective <foreign xml:lang="grc">ἀναίσθητος</foreign> (sensless, which does not belong to the realm of the senses) is connected to death in epitaphs — cf. <foreign xml:lang="grc">ἀναισθήτῳ περὶ τύμβῳ</foreign> in IG VII 2543 l. 2 — whereas here it refers to earthly life, from which a respite is requested) for Boniface himself, for his wife (referred to in ll. 10-11 as "fellow servant in Christ", on the expression <foreign xml:lang="grc">δοῦλος ἐν Χριστῷ</foreign>, see IG XIV 442, 629), and for his mother.</p>
            </div>
